Title: Restore 32-bit Windows (ia32) targeting
Upstream-Commit: 5517a93dec45 (2026-07-13) feat!: remove 32-bit Windows (ia32) and Linux ARM (armv7l) builds (#51816)
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

A partial revert of #51816, which is what ended 32-bit Windows at v43.x. Only
the parts that a 32-bit build or its artifacts need; the rest of that commit is
out of scope here:

  .github/** (20 files)        CI matrices. This repo builds with build.sh, not
                               Electron's workflows.
  spec/** (9 files)            removed ia32-specific skips in the test suite.
  README.md, docs/**           the announcement itself. docs/breaking-changes.md
                               keeps its entry: it is accurate about what
                               upstream publishes, which is not what we build.
  script/release/**            Electron's release uploaders. build.sh has its
                               own package(), which is where the ia32 naming
                               they describe is handled instead.
  fix_disable_mold_for_linux_arm32.patch, and the Linux-arm32 half of
  build_fix_profile_runtime_resolution_for_pgo_instrumented_builds.patch
                               Linux ARM32, the other platform that commit
                               dropped. Not a target here. (The Windows
                               cross-compile half of that PGO patch survived
                               upstream and we rely on it.)

Restored:

  script/lib/config.py         get_target_arch() mapping x86 -> ia32, the
                               architecture string every Electron download
                               resolver derives from process.arch. Without it
                               Electron's own tooling names 32-bit artifacts
                               win32-x86, which nothing looks for.
  script/zip_manifests/dist_zip.win.x86.manifest
  script/zip_manifests/chromedriver_zip.win.x86.manifest
                               the expected contents of both 32-bit zips,
                               restored verbatim from the commit. Note they
                               differ from the x64 manifests only by dropping
                               dxil.dll and dxcompiler.dll. Only
                               script/zip_manifests/check-zip-manifest.py reads
                               these, and only .github/actions/build-electron
                               runs it, so the build never consults them -- they
                               are here so that check can be run at all, and as
                               the record of what a correct 32-bit zip holds.

Nothing in the GN build had to be restored: #51816 never touched it, and the
32-bit code paths are all still there -- electron/BUILD.gn still sets
/STACK:0x80000 for current_cpu == "x86" (the fiber workaround for Chrome's
1.5 MiB main-thread stack), and //build/config/compiler/pgo/BUILD.gn still
resolves an Electron PGO target named win-x86. That last one is a dangling
branch: no win-x86.pgo.txt was ever published, so gn gen for x86 dies reading
it. assets/args/release_x86.gn sets chrome_pgo_phase = 0 to opt out, which is
the one way a 32-bit build is not simply a 64-bit build with another target_cpu.

diff --git a/electron/script/lib/config.py b/electron/script/lib/config.py
index a05fe1c..23fec07 100644
--- a/electron/script/lib/config.py
+++ b/electron/script/lib/config.py
@@ -27,6 +27,8 @@
   arch = os.environ.get('TARGET_ARCH')
   if arch is None:
     return 'x64'
+  if arch == 'x86':
+    return 'ia32'
   return arch
 
 def set_verbose_mode(mode):
